    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>The biggest myth of all is that rubbing your wrists together seals in the scent. It doesn't. In fact, it does the exact opposite: friction creates heat and shear stress that breaks down delicate top notes, crushing the fragrance before it even has a chance to bloom.</p><p>Here is what happens when you dismantle old misconceptions and start applying fragrance the way master perfumers intended.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "editorial--text-section",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Myth 1: Rubbing Wrists vs Myth 2: Expensive Equals Longer-Lasting",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p>When you spray perfume onto your wrists and rub them together vigorously, you generate thermal friction. That heat causes the volatile top notes—such as delicate bergamot, mandarin, and light florals—to evaporate prematurely.</p><p>Instead of experiencing a smooth 30-minute transition, you crush the opening and jump straight into the mid-notes. The fix is simple: spray, let the liquid air-dry for ten seconds, and let your natural body heat do the work.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p>Price tag has virtually zero correlation with longevity. Many £300 niche fragrances are formulated as light Eau de Colognes or delicate citrus florals designed to last only 3 to 4 hours by artistic choice.</p><p>Conversely, a well-formulated inspired Eau de Parfum with rich amber, cedarwood, or vanilla bases will comfortably last 10 hours. Longevity is determined by molecular weight and oil concentration, not price tags.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "editorial--content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>Myth 3: 100% natural ingredients are superior to synthetics</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>Many shoppers believe that a perfume made exclusively from natural botanical extracts is inherently higher quality. In truth, modern perfumery would not exist without safe, lab-developed aromatic molecules.</p><p>Natural citrus oils evaporate in minutes; synthetic fixatives like Ambroxan, Hedione, and Iso E Super give perfumes their incredible longevity, projection, and velvety texture. Furthermore, ethical synthetics replace animal-derived musks and protect endangered woods.</p><p>The highest quality perfumes use a harmonious balance of natural botanical essences and premium lab-crafted fixatives.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>Myth 4: Storing perfume in your bathroom vanity</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>Your bathroom is the worst possible place to store fine fragrance. The rapid temperature fluctuations and steamy humidity from daily showers cause the delicate chemical bonds in perfume oils to oxidise and spoil rapidly.</p><p>Store your bottles in a cool, dark place—like a bedroom wardrobe or dresser drawer. Kept away from direct sunlight and heat, high-concentration inspired perfumes will maintain their original beauty for years.</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
